If you use Claude to draft emails, product copy or graphics, some of that output may carry a machine-readable mark saying an AI was involved. Anthropic has documented how this works, and an EU date makes it worth understanding now. 2 December 2026 is when a grace period on the marking duty ends for providers of generative AI systems that were already on the market before the rules began to apply.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, which is also the company whose marking system is described here. It was checked by a separate AI reviewer, also a Claude model made by Anthropic, against the cited pages for figures, dates and wording; this is not a full human review. Stackbrook did not test the watermark or the checker and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The header image is AI-generated. This is general information, not legal advice.*

## Two kinds of mark

Anthropic describes two separate techniques.

**A text watermark.** When a supported model chooses between likely next words, a secret key and the preceding words settle the choice in place of an ordinary random number generator. Anthropic says the method is a version of SynthID-Text, published by Google DeepMind in 2024. According to the company, nothing is added to the text, there are no hidden characters, there are no extra tokens or extra cost, and meaning and readability are unchanged; those are the company's own claims, which we did not test. Because the pattern sits in the word choices, it travels with copied text. Anthropic says light edits probably will not remove it completely, while a rewrite that replaces every word will. We found no setting for switching the watermark off on the pages we read.

**Content Credentials on files.** When Claude creates a supported file, such as a PNG, JPEG or SVG image, it attaches a signed provenance note in the file's metadata using the open C2PA standard. This is a label attached to the file, not something hidden in the picture.

Anthropic says neither mark identifies you, your organisation or your chat.

## Which products and models

The Help Center lists the Claude Platform (API), the Claude apps, Claude Code, Claude Cowork and Claude Tag, worldwide, and notes that some platforms or features may not support every type of mark. Supported models reached through AWS, Google Cloud or Microsoft Foundry carry the text watermark too, although the table shows no cloud watermark for Opus 3. File credentials apply only where Claude can create files, which the page limits to the Claude apps and the API, including the Claude Platform on AWS and Claude in Microsoft Foundry.

Anthropic says models launched in the EU on or after 2 August 2026 are to support marking from launch, and its model table lists the Fable, Mythos, Opus, Sonnet and Haiku families, including several earlier releases. One footnote matters for developers. For Opus 3, Opus 4.5, Opus 4.6, Sonnet 4.5, Sonnet 4.6 and Haiku 4.5, changing temperature, top_p or top_k from their defaults can make the text watermark unreliable. The developer documentation says to leave those three settings out to keep the mark, and to check whether a framework or library adds them for you.

The pages also say Anthropic is still working on watermarking for models released before 2 August. Its explainer, first published on 14 August, said this would roll out over the coming months. They give no completion date, and we could not reconcile that wording with the table's listing of older models, so check the current table for the exact model you use.

## Who can check, and how

For files, Anthropic offers a free checker page. It accepts common image, video and audio formats, including JPG, PNG, WEBP, SVG, MP4 and MP3, up to 100 MB. The page states that the check runs in your browser, that the file never leaves your device and that it is not stored. It reads only the attached credential and does not examine text.

For text, there is no public checker on the pages we read. A detection API is in private preview for eligible organisations, listed as regulators, law enforcement, media, fact-checkers, independent researchers, educational organisations and EU civil society groups, plus select enterprises that must verify the watermark to meet their own duties under the Act. Anthropic says access will widen over time.

## What a result can and cannot tell you

This is the part to read before anyone acts on a result.

A detected mark is not fully conclusive. It indicates that Claude may have processed the content, which includes proofreading, translating, summarising or converting a file that a person originally wrote. Anthropic also says the watermark cannot tell the difference between text Claude wrote and text Claude heavily edited, and the content may have been changed afterwards.

A missing mark does not show that no AI was used. The listed reasons include:

- the model predates marking support;
- the text was later heavily edited, paraphrased, translated or mixed into other writing;
- the passage is very short, because confidence rises with length;
- file metadata was stripped by conversion, re-saving or a screenshot;
- the platform or file type does not support that mark;
- certain older models were called with non-default sampling settings.

Factual passages and light proofreading leave few word choices, so they carry a weaker signal. Code is similar, since the watermark is not applied where an exact output is required. The key also only speaks to Claude. It cannot identify text from another company's model, and Anthropic says other developers will use their own watermarks. Tools that guess at AI writing from style work differently.

We found no published detection accuracy or false-positive rate on these pages. Treat a result as one clue, not as grounds on its own to accuse a student, employee or supplier. Anthropic also says the watermark does not change who owns the output or your rights under its terms.

