## The EU dates behind it

Article 50(2) of the EU AI Act requires providers of AI systems that generate synthetic text, images, audio or video to mark that output in a machine-readable format and make it detectable, as far as technically feasible. There are exceptions, including tools that only assist with standard editing or do not substantially alter what you put in.

The Commission published a voluntary Code of Practice on 10 June 2026. Anthropic says it signed in July, as one of around 190 signatories. Article 50 applies from 2 August 2026. The Commission's FAQ says systems placed on the market before that day get a grace period for the marking and detection duty only, and must comply from 2 December 2026. The FAQ also says there is no duty to go back and label material made before 2 August. Anthropic applies its text watermark worldwide, saying it does not yet have a durable way to limit it by region.

## What a small business should check

A provider's invisible mark does not cover every duty that may fall on the business using the tool. The law calls that user a deployer, and the term does not cover purely personal, non-professional use. The December grace period covers only the marking and detection duty on providers. The deployer duties below have applied since 2 August 2026.

- **Deepfakes.** Deployers must disclose, visibly or audibly, image, audio or video content that counts as a deepfake. The FAQ says they cannot simply rely on the machine-readable mark. Creative and satirical works get a lighter form of disclosure.
- **Public-interest text.** AI-generated or manipulated text published to inform the public on matters of public interest must be disclosed. There is an exception where it has had human review or editorial control and a person or company holds editorial responsibility. Spell-checking alone does not count.
- **Your own AI products.** If you build a chatbot or generator and offer it to others, you may be a provider yourself. A chatbot provider must also make sure people know they are dealing with AI, unless that is obvious. Anthropic says builders should assess independently what Article 50 requires of their products.
- **Emotion and biometric tools.** Using emotion recognition or biometric categorisation systems carries a separate duty to inform the people exposed.
- **Client expectations.** If a contract promises human-written work, remember that even AI editing may leave a signal.

The FAQ describes fines of up to 15 million euros or 3% of worldwide annual turnover for breaches, and says proportionality may apply to small and medium firms. Whether any rule applies to your business depends on facts a general article cannot judge, so ask a qualified adviser if in doubt.
